WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.301 --> 00:00:02.836
-This is your Broadway debut.
Congratulations.

00:00:02.836 --> 00:00:03.936
It's really exciting.
-Thanks.

00:00:03.936 --> 00:00:05.538
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:05.538 --> 00:00:09.075
-And we had your co-star
Adam Driver here,

00:00:09.075 --> 00:00:11.110
and he was explaining, your --

00:00:11.110 --> 00:00:12.611
Basically, your friend
passes away,

00:00:12.611 --> 00:00:14.647
and it's about your relationship
with your friend's brother,

00:00:14.647 --> 00:00:16.115
who's played by Adam Driver.

00:00:16.115 --> 00:00:18.384
-Adam Driver. Right.

00:00:18.384 --> 00:00:20.219
-It's a very intense show.
-It's very intense.

00:00:20.219 --> 00:00:21.654
-And, again,
your Broadway debut.

00:00:21.654 --> 00:00:24.257
Do you have more respect
for stage actors,

00:00:24.257 --> 00:00:25.425
having done this for a little?

00:00:25.425 --> 00:00:29.261
-Oh, my God.
Who would do this? Literally.

00:00:29.261 --> 00:00:32.365
Like, who -- I mean,
I'm sure musical theater

00:00:32.365 --> 00:00:35.268
is much more fun, but,
like, to go and cry

00:00:35.268 --> 00:00:36.870
and get yelled at every night.

00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:40.640
-Yeah.
-"Here we go again. Let's go."

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:41.874
And like every day.

00:00:41.874 --> 00:00:44.010
Tuesday night,
you're like, "Okay.

00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:45.144
Here we go."

00:00:45.144 --> 00:00:46.278
-And it's so nice of you --
By the way,

00:00:46.278 --> 00:00:47.947
this is your one night to
not get yelled at,

00:00:47.947 --> 00:00:49.048
and you came
and spent it with us.

00:00:49.048 --> 00:00:50.182
I really appreciate it.

00:00:50.182 --> 00:00:51.684
-Well, I like you.
-It's your Monday off.

00:00:51.684 --> 00:00:54.620
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:56.922
As you do it more, are you
getting more used to it

00:00:56.922 --> 00:00:58.958
or is it still, every night,
just it's crazy to you

00:00:58.958 --> 00:01:01.461
that people do this
for a living?

00:01:01.461 --> 00:01:02.695
-It's still crazy to me.
-Yeah.

00:01:02.695 --> 00:01:03.763
-Still crazy to me.

00:01:03.763 --> 00:01:05.765
I mean, I'm a nervous
person anyway.

00:01:05.765 --> 00:01:07.901
It's not where I live, you know,

00:01:07.901 --> 00:01:09.601
like, being out
in front of people like,

00:01:09.601 --> 00:01:12.739
"Watch me cry in person."

00:01:12.739 --> 00:01:16.476
But it's -- You know, I have
immense respect for people

00:01:16.476 --> 00:01:18.911
who do it, and a beer
definitely helps.

00:01:18.911 --> 00:01:20.880
-A beer helps? That's good.
That's good to hear.

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:24.584
And then I'm always fascinated
by how people on Broadway stages

00:01:24.584 --> 00:01:26.553
talk about how an audience can
be completely different

00:01:26.553 --> 00:01:27.587
from one night to the next.

00:01:27.587 --> 00:01:28.921
-It's crazy.

00:01:28.921 --> 00:01:31.290
I mean, they really --
I mean, I sound like an idiot

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:32.592
because I guess everyone
knows that.

00:01:32.592 --> 00:01:34.226
But it really affects
your performance.

00:01:34.226 --> 00:01:37.596
I mean, sometimes, you'll have
a really loud, drunk crowd

00:01:37.596 --> 00:01:39.098
that, you know, when Adam
walks on they're, like,

00:01:39.098 --> 00:01:40.333
cat-calling him and stuff.

00:01:40.333 --> 00:01:43.069
We're like, "Should we wait
a few minutes?

00:01:43.069 --> 00:01:44.837
Did we get it all out?"

00:01:44.837 --> 00:01:46.405
Or, you know, one time --

00:01:46.405 --> 00:01:47.607
Or, sometimes,
they're very thoughtful

00:01:47.607 --> 00:01:50.209
and they're listening,
like, a matinee crowd.

00:01:50.209 --> 00:01:53.912
And then my favorite is --
there's a really great moment

00:01:53.912 --> 00:01:56.615
where there's a
Bruce Springsteen song in it.

00:01:56.615 --> 00:01:58.584
And Adam and I
are still basically

00:01:58.584 --> 00:01:59.953
doing the scene on stage.

00:01:59.953 --> 00:02:02.387
And the song comes on,
which I really love.

00:02:02.387 --> 00:02:03.990
And someone yelled out --

00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:07.793
This woman was like,
"Oh, I hate this song!"

00:02:07.793 --> 00:02:08.861
-It's amazing.

00:02:08.861 --> 00:02:10.329
-We're like,
"Should we turn it off?"

00:02:10.329 --> 00:02:14.533
-The level of entitlement that
people have at a Broadway show

00:02:14.533 --> 00:02:16.301
to let you know exactly
what they're feeling.

00:02:16.301 --> 00:02:18.304
-Like, it's unbelievable.

00:02:18.304 --> 00:02:20.039
But I guess that's, you know --
-That's just how it goes.

00:02:20.039 --> 00:02:22.141
-That's how it goes, but
that's kind of the fun of it.

00:02:22.141 --> 00:02:24.811
-Adam was also saying that
because he's Kylo Ren, as well,

00:02:24.811 --> 00:02:27.480
that there was -- One of the
nice things he was talking

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:28.981
about the audience is
there are people who maybe

00:02:28.981 --> 00:02:30.783
come to see him in a show
that wouldn't normally

00:02:30.783 --> 00:02:32.117
go to a Broadway show.

00:02:32.117 --> 00:02:34.988
-And I think our show has that,

00:02:34.988 --> 00:02:37.189
maybe differently than
some of the other shows.

00:02:37.189 --> 00:02:39.492
I think a lot of the people
we meet afterwards

00:02:39.492 --> 00:02:41.093
have never been
to a Broadway show.

00:02:41.093 --> 00:02:42.228
-That's a really
cool experience to give people.

00:02:42.228 --> 00:02:43.328
-It is cool.

00:02:43.328 --> 00:02:44.630
Yeah, and, I mean,
I didn't grow up

00:02:44.630 --> 00:02:47.667
getting to go to a lot of shows,
so I think it is cool.

00:02:47.667 --> 00:02:51.404
And, you know, it brings --
It's not exactly a light show.

00:02:51.404 --> 00:02:53.673
-Right.
-Although, it is very funny.

00:02:53.673 --> 00:02:55.775
I have to say,
Adam is very funny in it, too.

00:02:55.775 --> 00:02:57.910
He's kind of this big force,
but it's very funny.

00:02:57.910 --> 00:03:00.313
He plays it very funny, so --
-Nice tie-in to "force."

00:03:00.313 --> 00:03:02.448
-Yeah.
-Really nice.

00:03:02.448 --> 00:03:04.317
Well, I understand that you
have to do this now,

00:03:04.317 --> 00:03:06.886
because you're in
the new "Star Wars" movies,

00:03:06.886 --> 00:03:08.054
so you get a little scratch.

00:03:08.054 --> 00:03:09.922
You get a little scratch
when you say "force."

00:03:09.922 --> 00:03:11.090
It's really amazing.

00:03:11.090 --> 00:03:14.193
I will -- I almost don't
want to waste the time

00:03:14.193 --> 00:03:16.562
of trying to ask you about it,
like, 'cause every time

00:03:16.562 --> 00:03:18.564
there's somebody whose in one
of these big movies,

00:03:18.564 --> 00:03:20.133
be it an "Avengers" movie,
a "Star Wars" movie,

00:03:20.133 --> 00:03:22.368
I'm like, "You can't tell me
anything, right?"

00:03:22.368 --> 00:03:24.437
-But it is -- I just --
I wish I could swear,

00:03:24.437 --> 00:03:25.538
but you can't.

00:03:25.538 --> 00:03:27.540
But it is so...
-Go for it.

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:28.908
-...F'ing cool.
-Yeah, okay.

00:03:28.908 --> 00:03:32.278
-Like, it is, like, so --
And when I was there,

00:03:32.278 --> 00:03:35.748
I was thinking the whole -- I
mean, it's just the cost--

00:03:35.748 --> 00:03:37.549
Specifically my costume.

00:03:37.549 --> 00:03:39.184
I mean, it's just awesome.
It's so cool.

00:03:39.184 --> 00:03:40.953
-And I know we can
talk about this.

00:03:40.953 --> 00:03:43.256
We can talk about your history
with "Star Wars."

00:03:43.256 --> 00:03:47.860
Is it true that, growing up,
you were afraid of Yoda?

00:03:47.860 --> 00:03:49.562
Is this a true thing?

00:03:49.562 --> 00:03:52.865
-When I was in kindergarten,
my best friend's dad

00:03:52.865 --> 00:03:55.535
dressed up as Yoda,
but in a very realistic mask.

00:03:55.535 --> 00:03:56.970
Like, not just like,
"Oh, I'm Yoda."

00:03:56.970 --> 00:03:58.070
-Yeah.

00:03:58.070 --> 00:03:59.304
-Like, I mean,
in one of those real --

00:03:59.304 --> 00:04:01.440
-To the point where you
thought he might be Yoda?

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:02.841
-He might be Yoda.

00:04:02.841 --> 00:04:04.276
And, by the way,
he's a nice guy,

00:04:04.276 --> 00:04:05.511
so I don't -- But I cried.

00:04:05.511 --> 00:04:07.179
-You cried when
somebody walked in like Yoda.

00:04:07.179 --> 00:04:08.314
That's a lot.

00:04:08.314 --> 00:04:09.649
-Have you seen --
I mean, in kindergarten,

00:04:09.649 --> 00:04:10.683
you probably hadn't seen
"Star Wars" yet.

00:04:10.683 --> 00:04:12.285
-I know. I wonder. Maybe not.

00:04:12.285 --> 00:04:14.821
-Your introduction to Yoda
might have been a weird guy.

00:04:14.821 --> 00:04:16.322
-A scary man.
-Yeah.

00:04:16.322 --> 00:04:17.756
That's a hard way to meet Yoda.
-It was. It was.

00:04:17.756 --> 00:04:19.091
-Yeah.
-Now I know he's good.

00:04:19.091 --> 00:04:20.994
-But, over time, you're fine.
-Now I know he's good.

00:04:20.994 --> 00:04:22.829
Now I like him.
-Right. That's good.

00:04:22.829 --> 00:04:25.331
And, again, you can't give --
You can't divulge these secrets?

00:04:25.331 --> 00:04:26.732
-I can't.

00:04:26.732 --> 00:04:29.335
-But, yet, you -- And we've
talked about it before the show.

00:04:29.335 --> 00:04:30.369
You played one of the great
secret-keepers of all time

00:04:30.369 --> 00:04:31.504
in "The Americans."

00:04:31.504 --> 00:04:32.705
-It's true.

00:04:32.705 --> 00:04:34.040
-Do you think that's been
helpful for you now --

00:04:34.040 --> 00:04:36.175
-Yes. Yes.
-That training.

00:04:36.175 --> 00:04:38.745
-Yes. Definitely.
-That Russian spy training?

00:04:38.745 --> 00:04:40.313
-Yeah.

00:04:40.313 --> 00:04:43.015
-I have been fortunate enough to
see you since that show ended.

00:04:43.015 --> 00:04:44.416
-We saw each other.
-We did.

00:04:44.416 --> 00:04:47.720
And I complimented you and
Matthew both on the fact that

00:04:47.720 --> 00:04:50.556
a show that I think there is
this real expectation

00:04:50.556 --> 00:04:51.691
of this as one of those shows

00:04:51.691 --> 00:04:53.226
that's going to nail
the landing.

00:04:53.226 --> 00:04:55.394
And I think you guys
did nail the landing.

00:04:55.394 --> 00:04:56.762
That must have been
such a relief.

00:04:56.762 --> 00:04:58.931
-Such a --
'Cause no one ever does.

00:04:58.931 --> 00:05:02.568
Like, you're never lucky enough.
It's just the stars don't align.

00:05:02.568 --> 00:05:05.171
But these writers --
they kind of had the idea

00:05:05.171 --> 00:05:07.606
from the beginning,
and I love that ending.

00:05:07.606 --> 00:05:09.575
I mean, you never get out
while it's still good.

00:05:09.575 --> 00:05:12.911
-Yeah.
-And they did, and I loved it.

00:05:12.911 --> 00:05:16.148
-How do you --
What was your first interaction

00:05:16.148 --> 00:05:17.417
with that last script?

00:05:17.417 --> 00:05:18.451
Like, was it the table read

00:05:18.451 --> 00:05:20.185
or do you get to read it
on your own?

00:05:20.185 --> 00:05:21.854
-They send them to you
usually a little bit before.

00:05:21.854 --> 00:05:24.456
And I was actually behind
in reading my scripts.

00:05:24.456 --> 00:05:26.058
I was a couple episodes behind.

00:05:26.058 --> 00:05:28.794
So, I had this rare
three-hour break

00:05:28.794 --> 00:05:32.231
and I went to this restaurant
nearby where we were shooting

00:05:32.231 --> 00:05:34.733
and I sat with like
three of the last episodes

00:05:34.733 --> 00:05:37.937
and just burned through
them with a giant glass of wine.

00:05:37.937 --> 00:05:40.606
And I just cried
in this restaurant.

00:05:40.606 --> 00:05:41.741
I was doing one of those things
where you, like,

00:05:41.741 --> 00:05:43.442
cover your face.

00:05:43.442 --> 00:05:48.013
And I just --
It was so painful and so good.

00:05:48.013 --> 00:05:51.316
It was, like, the perfect
kind of payment

00:05:51.316 --> 00:05:52.851
that they had to give.

00:05:52.851 --> 00:05:55.821
-And, of course, one of
the legacies of that show

00:05:55.821 --> 00:05:57.590
will be -- That's where you
and Matthew met, right?

00:05:57.590 --> 00:05:59.558
-That's true.
-You have a child now.

00:05:59.558 --> 00:06:00.860
-We do.
Almost the same age as yours.

00:06:00.860 --> 00:06:02.395
-Almost the same age as ours.

00:06:02.395 --> 00:06:05.998
And is there anything -- If you
walk in to where you guys live,

00:06:05.998 --> 00:06:07.767
is it a monument to
"The Americans?"

00:06:07.767 --> 00:06:10.402
Is it just, like,
all the wigs in glass cases?

00:06:10.402 --> 00:06:11.404
Is there any --
-Yes. No.

00:06:11.404 --> 00:06:12.638
-Okay, good.
-No.

00:06:12.638 --> 00:06:15.073
The only thing
that we took, collectively,

00:06:15.073 --> 00:06:17.410
between Matthew and I --
He took --

00:06:17.410 --> 00:06:20.913
There was a kind of gross,
horrifying scene

00:06:20.913 --> 00:06:23.015
involving an ax in a garage
and a person's body.

00:06:23.015 --> 00:06:24.416
-Yep. Yeah.

00:06:24.416 --> 00:06:26.986
-And, obviously, we didn't use a
real ax on a person's body.

00:06:26.986 --> 00:06:28.254
-Right.

00:06:28.254 --> 00:06:31.357
-So there was a fake ax,
and that is what Matthew wanted.

00:06:31.357 --> 00:06:37.463
-That is --
I love Matthew, but I --

00:06:37.463 --> 00:06:40.232
If he told me that's what he was
bringing home, I'd move out.

00:06:40.232 --> 00:06:42.702
Yeah.
-A little disturbing.

00:06:42.702 --> 00:06:45.371
And our 2-year-old, who's
a little wild,

00:06:45.371 --> 00:06:46.638
comes in just,
like, wielding it.

00:06:46.638 --> 00:06:48.641
And you're like,
"No, no, no, no, no!"

00:06:48.641 --> 00:06:50.409
-And he's like, "You're the guys
that brought the ax home."

00:06:50.409 --> 00:06:51.411
-And I'm like, "It's true."

